Fillets: an end over pieces whose face at the end has its edge in two
pieces (mitcad#343).

A concave rounding between a bar's side and a cylinder it joins runs on
tangentially over the bar's rounded and chamfered top edge and ends at the
vertex where the chamfer, the cylinder and the cap meet. Its contact on
the cylinder reaches the cap first; the walk goes on past it until the
other contact crosses from the bar's rounding onto the chamfer, and its
extension over the chamfer (the cylinder's side given up, ChFi3d_Purge)
reaches the chamfer's edge with the cap: the end lies over two pieces,
both cut by the cap, as the bodies stored in the .f3d files have it.

MoreSurfdata takes such an end (PerformMoreSurfdata) where the point of
the piece before the last lies on an edge of the face at the end at the
vertex. Here the cap's edge with the cylinder is in two pieces: the
profile's vertex where the bar's side met the cylinder stays on it after
the chamfer and the rounding took the side's top away, with only those
two pieces at it. The point lies on the piece away from the vertex, the
end was not taken, and PerformOneCorner dropped the extension and failed
in PerformIntersectionAtEnd, whose walk round the bar's rounding, a face
without the vertex, did not come back ("the max number of faces reached").

ChFi3d_ArcGoesOnAs: an edge counts as the arc at the vertex where it goes
on from it between the same two faces through vertices where no other
edge meets; MoreSurfdata and PerformMoreSurfdata take it so.

Upstream: new.
